{"name":"Forest Green","hex":"#154406","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Forest Green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A place name:\nA suburb in Nailsworth parish, Stroud district, Gloucestershire, England, the home of Forest Green Rovers F.C. .\nA small village in Abinger parish, Mole Valley district, Surrey, England .\nA neighbourhood of Upton, Worcester County, Massachusetts, United States.\nAn unincorporated community (Forest Green Estates) in Perry Township, Shiawassee County, Michigan, United States.\nAn unincorporated community in Chariton County, Missouri, United States.","A suburb in Nailsworth parish, Stroud district, Gloucestershire, England, the home of Forest Green Rovers F.C. ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Forest_Green"}],"entries":[{"title":"O Little Town of Bethlehem","blocks":[{"text":"\"O Little Town of Bethlehem\" is a Christmas carol. Based on an 1868 text written by Phillips Brooks, the carol is popular on both sides of the Atlantic, but to different tunes: in the United States and Canada, to \"St. Louis\" by Brooks' collaborator, Lewis Redner; and in the United Kingdom and Ireland to \"Forest Green\", a tune collected by Ralph Vaughan Williams and first published in the 1906 English Hymnal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Manuscriptolittletownofbethlehem.png/330px-Manuscriptolittletownofbethlehem.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Little_Town_of_Bethlehem"},{"title":"Forest Green, Oh Forest Green","blocks":[{"text":"Forest Green, Oh Forest Green is the first single by American singer-songwriter Holly Miranda from her album The Magician's Private Library, released 9 November 2009 in the UK and 17 November 2009 in the United States by XL Recordings. It was co-written and features guest vocals by musician Brendan Coon."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a7/Forest_Green%2C_Oh_Forest_Green.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest_Green%2C_Oh_Forest_Green"},{"title":"Forest green","blocks":[{"text":"Forest green is a representation of the average color of the leaves of the trees of a temperate zone deciduous forest."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Forest_Green_%289674379239%29.jpg/330px-Forest_Green_%289674379239%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest_green"},{"title":"Forest","blocks":[{"text":"A forest is an ecosystem characterized by a dense community of trees. Hundreds of definitions of forest are used throughout the world, incorporating factors such as tree density, tree height, land use, legal standing, and ecological function. The United Nations' Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) defines a forest as, \"Land spanning more than 0.5 hectares with trees higher than 5 meters and a canopy cover of more than 10 percent, or trees able to reach these thresholds in situ. It does not include land that is predominantly under agricultural or urban use.\" Using this definition, Global Forest Resources Assessment 2025 found that forests covered 4.14 billion hectares, or approximately 31 percent of the world's land area in 2025."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/500px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/1280px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/500px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The heavily traversed Adirondack Mountains of Upstate New York form the southernmost part of the Eastern forest-boreal transition ecoregion, constituting part of the world's taiga biome.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg/500px-Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg/500px-Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Since the 13th century, the Niepołomice Forest in Poland has had special use and protection. In this view from space, different coloration can indicate different functions.[24]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANiepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Hellyer_Gorge%2C_Tasmania.jpg/500px-Hellyer_Gorge%2C_Tasmania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Hellyer_Gorge%2C_Tasmania.jpg/1280px-Hellyer_Gorge%2C_Tasmania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Hellyer_Gorge%2C_Tasmania.jpg/500px-Hellyer_Gorge%2C_Tasmania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Temperate rainforest in Tasmania's Hellyer Gorge","link":"https://en.wi